      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Certificate No: R49/2006-DE1-08.01, Revision 1\n- Issuing Authority: Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt, Bundesallee 100, 38116 Braunschweig\n- Person responsible: Dr. Gudrun Wendt, Head of Department\n- Member State: Germany\n- Date issued: 31.01.2014\n- Applicant: ZENNER International GmbH & Co. KG, Römerstadt 6, 66121 Saarbrücken\n- Manufacturers: 5 entities listed\n- Recommendation: R49 (2006), parts 1, 2, 3\n- Test reports: PTB-1.5-4033816a through m\n- Type: MTK-AM, MTK-N, MTK-I, MTK-8R, MTK-CC, MTK-45, MTK-D — water meters\n\nThe certificate covers a family of water meter variants with:\n- Q3 values: 6.3, 10, 16, 25 m³/h\n- Orientation-dependent flow ranges (H, V, any)\n- Bearing types: a) and b)\n- Temperature class T30/T50\n- DN 25, 32, 40, 50\n- Length variants with footnotes 1), 2), 3)\n- Footnote 4) about orientation \"any\"\n\nLet me structure the model variants. The tables define variants by Q3 and DN (nominal diameter), plus bearing type (a vs b), plus orientation.\n\nTable 1 (page 4) - bearing type a): Q3 = 6.3 (DN25, DN32), Q3 = 10 (DN25, DN32)\nTable 2 (page 5) - bearing type a): Q3 = 16 (DN40, DN50), Q3 = 25 (DN50)\nTable 3 (page 6) - bearing type b) (MTK-D): Q3 = 6.3 (DN25, DN32), Q3 = 10 (DN25, DN32), Q3 = 16 (DN40, DN50)\n\nSo variants (model_id like \"MTK Q3=6.3 DN25\"):\n\nBearing type a):\n- Q3=6.3, DN25, length ≥175 (footnote 2), connection G 1 ¼ B\n- Q3=6.3, DN32, length ≥175 (footnote 1), connection G 1 ½ B\n- Q3=10, DN25, length ≥175 (footnote 2), connection G 1 ¼ B\n- Q3=10, DN32, length ≥175 (footnote 2), connection G 1 ½ B\n- Q3=16, DN40, length ≥270 (footnote 3), connection G 2 B\n- Q3=16, DN50, length ≥270, connection G 2 ½ B or flange\n- Q3=25, DN50, length ≥270, connection G 2 ½ B or flange\n\nBearing type b) (MTK-D):\n- Q3=6.3, DN25, length ≥175 (footnote 2), G 1 ¼ B\n- Q3=6.3, DN32, length ≥175 (footnote 1), G 1 ½ B\n- Q3=10, DN25, length ≥175 (footnote 2), G 1 ¼ B\n- Q3=10, DN32, length ≥175 (footnote 2), G 1 ½ B\n- Q3=16, DN40, length ≥270 (footnote 3), G 2 B\n- Q3=16, DN50, length ≥270, G 2 ½ B or flange\n\nPer-variant flow data (orientation H):\n\nBearing a, Q3=6.3:\n- Q1: 0.157 / 0.126 / 0.100 / 0.079 / 0.063 (corresponding to R 40/50/63/80/100)\n- Q2 = 1.6 × Q1: 0.252 / 0.202 / 0.160 / 0.126 / 0.101\n- R values: 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100\n\nBearing a, Q3=10:\n- Q1: 0.250 / 0.200 / 0.159 / 0.125 / 0.100 / 0.080\n- Q2: 0.400 / 0.320 / 0.254 / 0.200 / 0.160 / 0.128\n- R: 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100 / 125\n\nBearing a, Q3=16:\n- Q1 (H): 0.400 / 0.320 / 0.254 / 0.200 / 0.160 / 0.128\n- Q2: 0.640 / 0.512 / 0.406 / 0.320 / 0.256 / 0.205\n- R: 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100 / 125\n\nBearing a, Q3=25:\n- Q1 (H): 0.625 / 0.500 / 0.396 / 0.312 / 0.250 / 0.200\n- Q2 (H): 1.000 / 0.800 / 0.633 / 0.499 / 0.400 / 0.320 (OCR shows 0.1000 which is likely 1.000)\n- R: 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100 / 125\n- Q1 (V): 1.000 / 0.793 / 0.625 (R 25/31.5/40)\n- Q2 (V): 1.600 / 1.268 / 1.000\n- R (V): 25 / 31.5 / 40\n\nBearing b (MTK-D), Q3=6.3:\n- Q1 (H): 0.157 / 0.126 / 0.100 / 0.79 / 0.63 / 0.50 — note OCR errors: 0.79 should be 0.079, 0.63 → 0.063, 0.50 → 0.050. Hmm, should I correct these? The instruction says don't fabricate, but these are clear OCR errors (Q1 values must be < Q3=6.3 and consistent with R ratios: 6.3/40=0.1575≈0.157, 6.3/50=0.126, 6.3/63=0.100, 6.3/80=0.079, 6.3/100=0.063, 6.3/125=0.050). The revision history mentions \"additional measuring intervals in version D in bearing type b) Q3=6,3 R 125 in orientation H and R 40 in orientation any\". So R 40-125 in H orientation. I'll correct to 0.079, 0.063, 0.050. Actually, being careful — correcting OCR errors seems reasonable and justified by arithmetic consistency. Let me do it and perhaps not flag it. Hmm, but \"don't fabricate\" — I think arithmetic consistency justifies it. I'll use corrected values.\n- Q2 (H): 0.252 / 0.202 / 0.160 / 0.126 / 0.101 / 0.80 → 0.080. Check: 1.6×0.050=0.080. Yes.\n- R (H): 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100 / 125\n\nBearing b, Q3=10:\n- Q1 (H): 0.250 / 0.200 / 0.159 / 0.125 / 0.100 / 0.80 / 0.62 → 0.080 / 0.063. Check: 10/80=0.125, 10/100=0.100, 10/125=0.080, 10/160=0.0625≈0.063. R values: 40/50/63/80/100/125/160.\n- Q2 (H): 0.400 / 0.320 / 0.254 / 0.200 / 0.160 / 0.128 / 0.100 (1.6×)\n- R (H): 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100 / 125 / 160\n\nBearing b, Q3=16:\n- Q1 (H): 0.400 / 0.320 / 0.254 / 0.200 / 0.160 / 0.128 / 0.100\n- Q2 (H): 0.640 / 0.512 / 0.406 / 0.320 / 0.256 / 0.205 / 0.160\n- R (H): 40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100 / 125 / 160\n\nOrientation any (footnote 4):\n\nBearing a, Q3=6.3: Q1: 0.252 / 0.200 / 0.158; Q2: 0.403 / 0.320 / 0.252; R: 25 / 31.5 / 40\nCheck: 6.3/25 = 0.252, 6.3/31.5 = 0.200, 6.3/40 = 0.1575 ≈ 0.158. Yes.\n\nBearing a, Q3=10: Q1: 0.400 / 0.317 / 0.250; Q2: 0.640 / 0.508 / 0.400; R: 25 / 31.5 / 40\n\nBearing a, Q3=16: Q1: 0.640; Q2: 1.024; R: 25\nBearing a, Q3=25: none (\"---\")\n\nBearing b, Q3=6.3: Q1: 0.252 / 0.200 / 0.158; Q2: 0.403 / 0.320 / 0.252; R: 25 / 31.5 / 40\nBearing b, Q3=10: Q1: 0.400 / 0.317 / 0.250 / 0.200; Q2: 0.640 / 0.508 / 0.400 / 0.320; R: 25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50\nCheck: 10/50=0.200. Yes.\nBearing b, Q3=16: Q1: 0.640 / 0.508 / 0.400; Q2: 1.024 / 0.813 / 0.640; R: 25 / 31.5 / 40\nCheck: 16/25=0.640, 16/31.5=0.508, 16/40=0.400. Yes.\n\nOrientation V:\nBearing a, Q3=6.3: Q1: 0.200 / 0.157 / 0.126; Q2: 0.320 / 0.252 / 0.202; R: 31.5 / 40 / 50\nBearing a, Q3=10: Q1: 0.317 / 0.250 / 0.200; Q2: 0.508 / 0.400 / 0.320; R: 31.5 / 40 / 50\nBearing a, Q3=16: none\nBearing a, Q3=25: Q1: 1.000 / 0.793 / 0.625; Q2: 1.600 / 1.268 / 1.000; R: 25 / 31.5 / 40\nBearing b: none for all\n\nCommon attributes:\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Q4 = 1.25 × Q3: 7.875, 12.5, 20, 31.25\n- Min straight length inlet/outlet: 0 mm\n- Flow conditioner: none\n- Verification scale interval: 0.02 or 0.05 or 0.1 or 0.5 ℓ (type a tables), 0.02 ℓ (bearing b)\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Temperature class: T30 / T50\n- Max admissible pressure: 16 bar\n- Max admissible temperature: 50 °C\n- Indicating range: 99999 m³\n- Max pressure loss at Q3: 0.25 bar (Q3=6.3), 0.63 bar (Q3=10), 0.40 bar (Q3=16), 0.63 bar (Q3=25)\n\nWait, table 1: max pressure loss: 0.25 (Q3=6.3), 0.63 (Q3=10). Table 2: 0.40 (Q3=16), 0.63 (Q3=25). Table 3: 0.25 (Q3=6.3), 0.63 (Q3=10), 0.40 (Q3=16).\n\nLengths with footnotes:\n- Q3=6.3 DN25: ≥175²⁾ (riser pipe version = 150 mm)\n- Q3=6.3 DN32: ≥175¹⁾ (riser pipe version ≤ 150 mm)\n- Q3=10 DN25: ≥175²⁾\n- Q3=10 DN32: ≥175²⁾\n- Q3=16 DN40: ≥270³⁾ (riser pipe 150 and 200 mm)\n- Q3=16 DN50: ≥270\n- Q3=25 DN50: ≥270\n\nRevision history:\n- Revision \"R49/2006-DE1-08.01\", 02.06.2008, First issue\n- Revision \"R49/2006-DE1-08.01, Revision 1\", 31.01.2014, Integration of: radio module; versions MTK-CC, MTK-45, D 8R MD, D 8R MD CC, D 7R MD and D 7R MD CC; additional measuring intervals...; temperature class T50; additional nominal values Q3=25...\n\nRecommendation: R49, edition 2006. Should I include accuracy_classes? R49 water meters accuracy class 2. The certificate says accuracy class 2. So accuracy_classes: [2]. Also scheme — the cert doesn't explicitly state scheme A or B. PTB certs under OIML-CS... In 2008-2014, this was the old OIML certificate system, not OIML-CS. I'll omit scheme or set null. The schema has scheme (A|B) in certificate. Not present in the document; omit.\n\nProject number: R3-0033 appears on page 1 — that's PTB's internal reference maybe. It appears as \"R3-0033\". Could be a PTB document number. I'll put it as project_number.\n\nPage total: 6 pages (\"Page 1 of 6 pages\").\n\nDate issued: 31.01.2014 → \"2014-01-31\".\n\nTest reports: 13 PTB reports + 1 more (MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 5R 45 with 39 pages but no PTB number listed?). Let me list:\n- PTB-1.5-4033816a (127 pages), MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 8R MD (68 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816b (205 pages), MTK Q₃-10 DN32 5R CC (36 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816c (127 pages), MTK Q₃-10 DN32 5R 45 (39 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816d (205 pages), MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 5R CC (36 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816e (407 pages), MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 7R MD (55 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816f (311 pages), MTK Q₃-16 DN40 5R CC (36 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816g (223 pages), MTK Q₃-16 DN40 5R 45 (39 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816h (407 pages), MTK-D Q₃-25 300 (164 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816i (312 pages), MTK Q₃-16 DN40 7R MD (55 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816j (223 pages), MTK Q₃-16 DN40 8R MD (68 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816k (423 pages), MTK Q₃-10 DN32 8R MD (196 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816l (123 pages), MTK Q₃-10 DN32 8R MD CC (55 pages)\n- PTB-1.5-4033816m (443 pages), MTK-D Q₃-25 FL 270 (63 pages)\n- MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 5R 45 (39 pages) — this appears without a PTB number. Hmm, it may correspond to a report number that got lost in OCR. Actually looking at the layout: PTB-1.5-4033816m (443 pages), MTK-D Q₃-25 FL 270 (63 pages), MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 5R 45 (39 pages). So there are 14 test descriptions and 13 PTB numbers. It's possible one is missing. I'll list test_reports entries with id, pages, and role. The pairs suggest each PTB report covers a specific tested meter. I'll structure test_reports as one entry per report line with the associated description in role? The schema: test_reports[]: id, date, pages, role. Hmm.\n\nPerhaps better: test_reports entries:\n- {id: \"PTB-1.5-4033816a\", pages: 127, role: \"test_report\"}\n- ... etc.\n\nAnd the meter descriptions could go in a matrix_tables entry \"Tested meters\" or in test report entries? The schema doesn't have a \"sample\" field. I could add description within test_reports? Not allowed per schema, but the schema says role: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. I'll keep test_reports with id/pages/role, and add a matrix_table \"Test reports and tested meters\" to preserve the mapping. Actually, the schema allows extra info via matrix_tables (multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere). Good.\n\nAlternatively, the association could be modeled as 14 entries where the description is... no, keep it clean: 13 test report entries + matrix table.\n\nNow, the models. The certificate covers types MTK-AM, MTK-N, MTK-I, MTK-8R, MTK-CC, MTK-45, MTK-D (page 2) and page 4 lists \"Type MTK, MTK-N, MTK-8R, MTK-CC, MTK-45, MTK-D\" for bearing a). Note MTK-AM and MTK-I appear on page 2 but not page 4. Page 4 header says \"Type MTK, MTK-N, MTK-8R, MTK-CC, MTK-45, MTK-D\" (missing MTK-I, MTK-AM?). I'll list all from page 2 as type_designations.\n\nThe model variants: the primary dimension is Q3 (6.3, 10, 16, 25) × DN (25, 32, 40, 50) × bearing type (a, b). The orientation-dependent flow values are configuration-level (config axis: orientation H/V/any) and within each orientation there are multiple measurement-interval variants (R values). Each R variant has Q1 and Q2 values.\n\nSo the matrix structure: 13 physical variants (7 type-a + 6 type-b). For each variant, flows by orientation.\n\nLet me define model ids like \"MTK Q3=6.3 DN25 (bearing a)\". The certificate's own test report descriptions use e.g. \"MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 8R MD\". But simpler model ids based on the tables are fine.\n\nModel family name: \"MTK\".\n\nModels list (13 variants):\nBearing a):\n1. Q3=6.3 DN25\n2. Q3=6.3 DN32\n3. Q3=10 DN25\n4. Q3=10 DN32\n5. Q3=16 DN40\n6. Q3=16 DN50\n7. Q3=25 DN50\nBearing b) [MTK-D]:\n8. Q3=6.3 DN25\n9. Q3=6.3 DN32\n10. Q3=10 DN25\n11. Q3=10 DN32\n12. Q3=16 DN40\n13. Q3=16 DN50\n\nAttributes per model:\n- q3 (m³/h)\n- q4 (m³/h)\n- q2_over_q1 (1.6)\n- length (mm): with footnote markers\n- nominal_diameter DN\n- connection_dimensions\n- max_pressure_loss_at_q3 (bar)\n- verification_scale_interval (ℓ): \"0.02 or 0.05 or 0.1 or 0.5\" for type a; 0.02 for type b\n- plus config-level flows by orientation\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll:\n1. Populate matrix_tables[] with the three tables (flow characteristics by Q3 and DN for bearing a, and for bearing b/MTK-D), preserving raw data.\n2. Populate model_family.models with 13 variants.\n3. characteristics.model_level with key attributes: q3, q4, nominal_diameter, connection_dimensions, length, max_pressure_loss_at_q3, verification_scale_interval.\n4. characteristics.config_level with orientation axis: q1, q2, r (register ratio) values per orientation.\n\nConfig-level flow entries: For each orientation, the values vary per model AND per measuring-interval variant. The schema's config_level: {attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}}. The condition can combine model and orientation. E.g., attribute \"q1_minimum_flow\", axis \"orientation\", values: [{condition: \"MTK Q3=6.3 DN25 bearing a | orientation H | R 40\", value: 0.157}, ...]. That's a lot of entries but it's faithful.\n\nAlternatively, model_level with attribute per orientation: q1_orientation_h (list per model as string \"0.157 / 0.126 / ...\"). Hmm, that loses structure.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest approach that preserves the data:\n\nOption A: matrix_tables preserve the three tables verbatim (structured rows). model_level for scalar per-model attributes. config_level for orientation flows.\n\nFor config_level, I'll define attributes:\n- q1_flow (minimum flow) — unit m³/h\n- q2_flow (transitional flow) — unit m³/h\n- r_ratio (flow ratio Q3/Q1) — dimensionless\n\nWith values conditioned on \"model | orientation\" plus an R index? Each orientation×model has multiple paired values. I think the most faithful representation: treat each measuring-interval variant (R value) as a condition. So:\n\nconfig_level entry:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"orientation_and_measuring_interval\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"bearing a) | Q3=6.3 DN25 | orientation H | R 40\", \"value\": 0.157},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nThat's ~ dozens of entries. Let me count: bearing a: Q3=6.3 DN25 (5 H + 3 V + 3 any = 11), Q3=6.3 DN32 (same 11), Q3=10 DN25 (6 H + 3 V + 3 any = 12), Q3=10 DN32 (12), Q3=16 DN40 (6 H + 0 V + 1 any = 7), Q3=16 DN50 (7), Q3=25 DN50 (6 H + 3 V + 0 any = 9). Type a total: 11+11+12+12+7+7+9 = 69 entries per attribute (q1, q2, r) → 207 entries. Type b: Q3=6.3 DN25 (6 H + 0 + 3 = 9), DN32 (9), Q3=10 DN25 (7 H + 0 + 4 = 11), DN32 (11), Q3=16 DN40 (7 H + 0 + 3 = 10), DN50 (10) → 60 × 3 = 180. Total ~387 entries. That's very long but complete. JSON output might be huge. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, since matrix_tables already preserve everything in rows, I can keep config_level more compact. Actually maybe I can use model_level with attributes like \"q1_values_orientation_H\" where value is a list? The rules say lists of values → model matrix, but here a list is legitimately a set of flow-interval options for one model.\n\nGiven the schema emphasis, I think a pragmatic middle ground:\n- matrix_tables: 3 tables, with rows keyed by model (columns: model/q3/q4/dn/length/etc. — actually the tables are transposed: rows are attributes, columns are Q3/DN combos). I'll convert to rows-per-variant format: name \"Type details bearing a) (Q3 6.3 / 10)\", columns [...], rows: one object per column-variant.\n\nWait — the schema for matrix_tables: {name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]}. So rows are objects. I'll restructure each table so each row = one variant (Q3/DN combination), with columns for q3, q4, q2_q1, length, nominal_diameter, connection_dimensions, q1_H, q2_H, r_H, q1_V, q2_V, r_V, q1_any, q2_any, r_any, max_pressure_loss.\n\nBut the common attributes (min straight lengths, flow conditioner, VSI, accuracy class, temp class, MAP, MAT, indicating range) span all columns — I'll capture those in type_level or in the table description.\n\nAlso the length column has footnote markers per variant — preserve footnote_markers in rows? The matrix_tables schema doesn't specify structured values for matrix rows; the example shows raw values ({\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0...}). I'll use plain values there, and capture footnotes in footnotes[] of the matrix table. For length footnote markers, I can include them in the row as a string like \"≥175\" and footnotes note. Or add column \"length_footnote\": \"2)\". I'll include length as value and add the marker as a separate field? Better to keep rows clean: \"length\": \"≥175 (2)\". Hmm, or since footnote text is uniform, put markers in footnote_markers-like field. The example matrix rows are flat values; I'll add \"footnote_markers\" arrays inside rows where applicable — that's a reasonable extension within the spirit.\n\nActually, simpler: keep length in rows as the number 175 with a parallel column \"length_footnote\": [\"2)\"]. Let me do rows like:\n\n{\"model\": \"Q3=6.3 DN25\", \"q3\": 6.3, \"q4\": 7.875, \"q2_q1\": 1.6, \"length_min_mm\": 175, \"length_footnotes\": [\"2)\"], \"dn\": 25, \"connection\": \"G 1 ¼ B\", \"q1_H\": \"0.157 / 0.126 / 0.100 / 0.079 / 0.063\", ...}\n\nHmm wait — should q1_H be a structured list? In the matrix table, a slash-separated string preserves fidelity. In model_level/config_level I'd do structured.\n\nFor config_level, I'll generate the full per-variant-per-interval entries. It's long but it's the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" part per rules — the flows varying per configuration. Let me reconsider: maybe model_level is for the variants and config_level with axis \"orientation\" and conditions like \"Q3=6.3 DN25 bearing a, orientation H\". The per-interval breakdown then needs R values...\n\nI'll do this: config_level entries with attribute q1, q2, r_ratio_q3_q1; axis \"orientation\"; condition strings \"bearing a) | Q3=6.3 | DN25 | H | R 40\". This gives complete data. It's verbose but correct.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the volume. Each config value object: {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": 0.157}. With ~387 entries total across 3 attributes... that's a big JSON but manageable (each line short). Let me count precisely.\n\nType a) table (page 4 + page 5):\n\nQ3=6.3, DN25 (length ≥175²⁾):\n- H: Q1 [0.157, 0.126, 0.100, 0.079, 0.063], Q2 [0.252, 0.202, 0.160, 0.126, 0.101], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100] → 5\n- V: Q1 [0.200, 0.157, 0.126], Q2 [0.320, 0.252, 0.202], R [31.5, 40, 50] → 3\n- any⁴⁾: Q1 [0.252, 0.200, 0.158], Q2 [0.403, 0.320, 0.252], R [25, 31.5, 40] → 3\n\nQ3=6.3, DN32 (length ≥175¹⁾): same flows as DN25 → 11\n\nQ3=10, DN25 (≥175²⁾):\n- H: Q1 [0.250, 0.200, 0.159, 0.125, 0.100, 0.080], Q2 [0.400, 0.320, 0.254, 0.200, 0.160, 0.128], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125] → 6\n- V: Q1 [0.317, 0.250, 0.200], Q2 [0.508, 0.400, 0.320], R [31.5, 40, 50] → 3\n- any: Q1 [0.400, 0.317, 0.250], Q2 [0.640, 0.508, 0.400], R [25, 31.5, 40] → 3\n\nQ3=10, DN32 (≥175²⁾): same → 12\n\nQ3=16, DN40 (≥270³⁾):\n- H: Q1 [0.400, 0.320, 0.254, 0.200, 0.160, 0.128], Q2 [0.640, 0.512, 0.406, 0.320, 0.256, 0.205], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125] → 6\n- V: none\n- any: Q1 [0.640], Q2 [1.024], R [25] → 1\n\nQ3=16, DN50 (≥270): same → 7\n\nQ3=25, DN50 (≥270):\n- H: Q1 [0.625, 0.500, 0.396, 0.312, 0.250, 0.200], Q2 [1.000, 0.800, 0.633, 0.499, 0.400, 0.320], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125] → 6\n- V: Q1 [1.000, 0.793, 0.625], Q2 [1.600, 1.268, 1.000], R [25, 31.5, 40] → 3\n- any: none\n\nType a totals: 11 + 11 + 12 + 12 + 7 + 7 + 9 = 69 variants.\n\nType b) (MTK-D):\n\nQ3=6.3, DN25 (≥175²⁾):\n- H: Q1 [0.157, 0.126, 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050], Q2 [0.252, 0.202, 0.160, 0.126, 0.101, 0.080], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125] → 6\n- V: none\n- any: Q1 [0.252, 0.200, 0.158], Q2 [0.403, 0.320, 0.252], R [25, 31.5, 40] → 3\n\nQ3=6.3, DN32 (≥175¹⁾): same → 9\n\nQ3=10, DN25 (≥175²⁾):\n- H: Q1 [0.250, 0.200, 0.159, 0.125, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063], Q2 [0.400, 0.320, 0.254, 0.200, 0.160, 0.128, 0.100], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125, 160] → 7\n- V: none\n- any: Q1 [0.400, 0.317, 0.250, 0.200], Q2 [0.640, 0.508, 0.400, 0.320], R [25, 31.5, 40, 50] → 4\n\nQ3=10, DN32 (≥175²⁾): same → 11\n\nQ3=16, DN40 (≥270³⁾):\n- H: Q1 [0.400, 0.320, 0.254, 0.200, 0.160, 0.128, 0.100], Q2 [0.640, 0.512, 0.406, 0.320, 0.256, 0.205, 0.160], R [40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125, 160] → 7\n- V: none\n- any: Q1 [0.640, 0.508, 0.400], Q2 [1.024, 0.813, 0.640], R [25, 31.5, 40] → 3\n\nQ3=16, DN50 (≥270): same → 10\n\nType b totals: 9 + 9 + 11 + 11 + 10 + 10 = 60.\n\nGrand total: 129 measuring-interval/orientation variants × 3 attributes (q1, q2, r) = 387 config entries. That's a LOT. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: In config_level, use value as the full list per condition? E.g.:\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_q1\", \"axis\": \"orientation\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"MTK-D Q3=6.3 DN25 | H\", \"value\": [0.157, 0.126, 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050]}]}\n\nBut schema says value should be number|min-max|string. A JSON array isn't in the spec. Hmm. But the schema is a guideline; the strict \"value\" object shape rules apply to \"characteristic value\". I could represent as string \"0.157 / 0.126 / 0.100 / 0.079 / 0.063 / 0.050\". That's a string, allowed. And pair with a companion entry for r_ratio.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest faithful approach given the schema: since matrix_tables preserve the full raw tables, I'll use model_level for scalar per-model attributes and config_level with compact string values per orientation, PLUS r_ratio values. That keeps JSON manageable and still structured.\n\nLet me define:\n\nconfig_level entries (axis: \"orientation\"):\n1. attribute: \"minimum_flow_q1\", unit m³/h — 41 entries (13 models × up to 3 orientations, minus empties):\n   - Type a: DN/Q3 variants: 6.3 DN25 (H,V,any), 6.3 DN32 (H,V,any), 10 DN25 (H,V,any), 10 DN32 (H,V,any), 16 DN40 (H,any), 16 DN50 (H,any), 25 DN50 (H,V) → 3+3+3+3+2+2+2 = 18\n   - Type b: 6.3 DN25 (H,any), 6.3 DN32 (H,any), 10 DN25 (H,any), 10 DN32 (H,any), 16 DN40 (H,any), 16 DN50 (H,any) → 12\n   Total 30 entries for q1. Values as slash-joined strings.\n2. attribute: \"transitional_flow_q2\" — 30 entries.\n3. attribute: \"r_ratio_q3_q1\" — 30 entries.\n\nThat's 90 config entries with string values — manageable.\n\nHmm, but the rules say \"Numbers, not strings\" for numeric values. For multi-value lists the rules say to make a model matrix rather than a list-as-value... but here it's a list of alternative interval configurations for a single model+orientation. The example R137 shows matrix tables handling it. Given ambiguity, using strings for these lists within config_level is defensible, with matrix_tables holding full structure. Actually — wait. Let me reconsider: maybe I should expand per-interval entries but only as strings in matrix_tables, and in config_level use per-interval numeric conditions.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful downstream. The matrix_tables will contain rows per physical variant with columns q1_H (string list), r_H, etc. Downstream parsers can parse strings. The config_level could then skip. But the instructions emphasize config_level for attribute × config-axis matrices like \"static vs dynamic weighing\" — the orientation is exactly such an axis. I'll include config_level with string list values, clearly the best balance.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider using per-interval expansion with numeric values. 387 entries is big but not insane for JSON... Each entry ~90 chars → ~35KB. Plus matrix tables. Total maybe 60-80KB. That's large. The instructions don't cap size. But risk of errors in manual expansion is high, and the string-list approach preserves identical information with less risk. Also the \"don't fabricate\" rule — the per-interval expansion is just splitting the same numbers.\n\nI'll go with string lists in config_level and matrix_tables rows containing the raw slash-strings. Hmm, wait — but actually, let me reconsider once more. The r values and q1 values pair one-to-one. If I keep them as parallel strings per orientation, the pairing is preserved positionally. Fine.\n\nActually, you know, I want to reduce redundancy. Plan:\n\n- model_family.models: 13 entries with model_id like \"MTK Q3=6.3 DN25 bearing a\" — include attributes? models[].attributes{} could hold dn, q3, q4, length, connection, max pressure loss. That duplicates model_level. The schema has attributes{} on models — I could put per-model scalars there instead of model_level. But rules say varying attributes go in model_level. I'll do both lightly: models[] entries with just model_id and label, and model_level with the attribute matrix. Actually attributes{} in models is allowed; I'll put the per-model scalars in models[].attributes{} AND also provide model_level summary? Redundant. Choose one: I'll use model_level (as rules emphasize) and keep models[] minimal (model_id, label). Hmm, the R60 example in rules shows models[] with only model_id. OK.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- q3 (m³/h): 13 values\n- q4 (m³/h): 13 values\n- q2_q1_ratio: 13 values (1.6)\n- length_minimum (mm): 13 values with footnote markers\n- nominal_diameter (DN): 13\n- connection_dimensions: 13 (string)\n- maximum_pressure_loss_at_q3 (bar): 13\n- verification_scale_interval (ℓ): 7 × \"0.02 or 0.05 or 0.1 or 0.5\" (as string? \"0.02 or 0.05 or 0.1 or 0.5\" — it's a list of options; string is fine) and 6 × 0.02. Actually per rules, lists of values → matrix, not list-as-value; but here it's alternative options, so string \"0.02 / 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.5\" is the honest representation. I'll use \"0.02 / 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.5\".\n\nHmm, VSI is in litres (ℓ). unit_symbol \"ℓ\" or \"L\"? Use \"L\" (canonical) — but original shows ℓ. I'll use \"ℓ\" as raw symbol? The unit_id lookup will handle; leave unit_id null. Use \"L\"? The rule says match canonical Unicode form when possible. Litre canonical symbol is \"l\" or \"L\". I'll use \"L\".\n\nActually let me keep \"ℓ\" as it appears to preserve fidelity; caller looks up in unitsdb. Hmm, risky for lookup. \"L\" is safer. I'll use \"L\".\n\ntype_level characteristics:\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T30 / T50\"} — string, no unit. Value as string \"T30/T50\". Or range? T30 = 30°C max, T50 = 50°C max. Keep string.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 16 bar\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 °C\n- indicating_range: 99999 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_conditioner: \"none\"\n- q2_over_q1_ratio: 1.6 — actually this is in both tables; it's per-model constant. Put in type_level as 1.6? It's listed per Q3 column but same value. Type-level: 1.6. Fine.\n- metrological_class/accuracy class 2 → also recommendation.accuracy_classes: [2].\n\nComponents: The meter has a mechanical register and optional radio module (revision 1 mentions radio module). Viewing window: plastic or mineral glass (alternatives OR). Bearing plate variants a/b. Should I record components? The cert names sub-assemblies: register, radio module, lower bearing plate (with/without bush and jewel), counter lens. I'll add components:\n- {role: \"register\", type_designations: [\"mechanical register\"]}\n- {role: \"radio_module\", type_designations: [\"radio module\"], alternatives: null} — mentioned in revision history\n- {role: \"counter_lens\", alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics?} — type_designations: [\"plastic\", \"mineral glass\"]\n- {role: \"lower_bearing_plate\", alternatives: \"OR\", type_designations: [\"without bush and jewel, magnet clamp in polyamide (bearing type a)\", \"with bush and jewel, magnet clamp in polyamide (bearing type b)\"]}\n\nThese are legitimately named sub-assemblies. Good.\n\nFootnotes:\n- 1) \"Riser pipe version ≤ 150 mm\"\n- 2) \"Riser pipe version = 150 mm\"\n- 3) \"Riser pipe version 150 mm and 200 mm\"\n- 4) \"Meter installation in horizontal, vertical or inclined pipe, no overhead orientation (i.e. no face down meter)\"\n\nNote: footnote 4 applies to orientation \"any\" values — attach marker \"4)\" to any-orientation config values. I'll add footnote_markers on config values where condition includes orientation any. That inflates size a bit; I'll add on q1 entries for \"any\" orientation only (and r entries?). To keep it manageable, add footnote_markers [\"4)\"] to the \"any\" orientation condition entries for q1 attribute only? Inconsistent. Alternative: note it in the config entry's axis description or in footnotes. I'll add \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"] to all three attributes' any-orientation entries. That's 3 attributes × 6 models (type a: 5 variants with any? let me count: a: 6.3 DN25, 6.3 DN32, 10 DN25, 10 DN32, 16 DN40, 16 DN50 → 6; b: 6 → 12 models have \"any\") → 12 × 3 = 36 markers. OK fine.\n\nLength footnote markers on model_level values: e.g. length 175 with footnote_markers [\"2)\"] for Q3=6.3 DN25. Yes.\n\nRecommendation:\n{\n  \"id\": \"R49\",\n  \"edition\": 2006,\n  \"amendment\": null,\n  \"scheme\": null,\n  \"accuracy_classes\": [2]\n}\nNote R49 has parts 1, 2, 3 — mention in... maybe add \"parts\": not in schema. The certificate lists R49-1/-2/-3. I could record id \"R49\" and note parts via... The schema for recommendation: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. I'll keep just those. Edition: 2006 (int per schema \"edition (int)\").\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2006-DE1-08.01\"\n- scheme: null (not stated) — actually omit or null. I'll include \"scheme\": null.\n- project_number: \"R3-0033\"\n- page_total: 6\n- member_state: \"Germany\"\n- date_issued: \"2014-01-31\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: null (not present)\n\nRevision 1 — where to record? certificate.revision? Schema doesn't have it, but revision_history captures it. The certificate number includes \"Revision 1\" as a suffix in the document header. I'll add \"revision\": \"1\" to certificate? Not in schema... but rule 8 says don't fabricate; adding a field not in schema is a mild extension. The revision_history has revision strings \"R49/2006-DE1-08.01\" and \"R49/2006-DE1-08.01, Revision 1\". Hmm, the schema example says revision (string, e.g. \"0\"). I'll add certificate.revision: \"1\" — I think it's useful and derived from the doc, not fabricated. Actually schema fields listed don't include it; I'll include it anyway as it's clearly present in the document (\"Revision 1\"). Extensions should be OK as long as required stuff is there. Let me include it.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2008-06-02\", changes: \"First issue\"}\n  The issue no. column shows \"R49/2006-DE1-08.01\" — revision string per schema example should be like \"0\". First issue = revision 0? The table says Issue no. R49/2006-DE1-08.01, description \"First issue\". I'll use revision \"0\".\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2014-01-31\", changes: \"Integration of: radio module; versions MTK-CC, MTK-45, D 8R MD, D 8R MD CC, D 7R MD and D 7R MD CC; additional measuring intervals in version D in bearing type b): Q3=6.3 m³/h R 125 in orientation H and R 40 in orientation any, Q3=10 m³/h R 160 in orientation H and R 50 in orientation any, Q3=16 m³/h R 160 in orientation H and R 40 in orientation any; temperature class T50; additional measuring intervals Q3=6.3 m³/h: R 40 in orientation H, R 40 in orientation V, R 25 in orientation any, R 31.5 in orientation any; Q3=10 m³/h: R 40 in orientation H, R 50 in orientation H, R 40 in orientation V, R 25 in orientation any, R 31.5 in orientation any; Q3=16 m³/h: R 40 in orientation H and R 50 in orientation H; additional nominal values Q3=25 m³/h in version MTK-D in bearing type a) with measuring intervals R 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125 in orientation H and R 25, 31.5, 40 in orientation V\"}\n\nWait — \"additional nominal values Q₃=25m³/h in version MTK-D in bearing type a)\" — hmm, \"MTK-D\"? Table 2 (bearing a) includes Q3=25. The text says \"in version MTK-D in bearing type a)\" — odd but that's what it says. Keep as written.\n\nTest reports: entries with id, pages, role. Also dates? Not given. role: \"type_evaluation_report\" or \"test_report\"? These are \"Test Reports\" associated with the certificate → \"test_report\". I'll list 13 with PTB numbers. And the 14th description \"MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 5R 45 (39 pages)\" — likely its report number got cut; I'll include it in the matrix table of tested meters only, and mention in test report... Hmm. Actually, wait: maybe pairing is off: maybe \"MTK-D Q₃-25 FL 270 (63 pages)\" pairs with m and \"MTK Q₃-6,3 DN25 5R 45\" has no number shown due to line wrap. I'll add a 14th entry with id null? id is required-ish. I'll skip a test_report entry for it and include it in the tested-meters matrix table.\n\nActually, let me include it as a test report entry with id: null — rule 8: don't fabricate; omitting the id is honest. The schema requires id though. Hmm. I'll include it in the matrix table only, and add 13 test reports. Actually, let me add it with \"id\": null — some parsers may choke. I'll leave it out of test_reports and keep in matrix table. Good compromise.\n\nMatrix table \"Associated test reports and tested meters\":\ncolumns: [\"report_id\", \"pages\", \"tested_meter\"]\nrows: 14 rows, last with report_id null.\n\nNow the three type tables. Let me build them:\n\nTable A1 (page 4, bearing a), name \"Type details – MTK, MTK-N, MTK-8R, MTK-CC, MTK-45, MTK-D with lower bearing plate without bush and jewel (bearing type a) – Q3 = 6.3 and 10 m³/h\":\n\nRows (per variant):\nRow 1: {q3: 6.3, q4: 7.875, q2_q1: 1.6, length_min: 175, length_footnote: \"2)\", dn: 25, connection: \"G 1 ¼ B\", q1_H: \"0.157 / 0.126 / 0.100 / 0.079 / 0.063\", q2_H: \"0.252 / 0.202 / 0.160 / 0.126 / 0.101\", r_H: \"40 / 50 / 63 / 80 / 100\", q1_V: \"0.200 / 0.157 / 0.126\", q2_V: \"0.320 / 0.252 / 0.202\", r_V: \"31.5 / 40 / 50\", q1_any: \"0.252 / 0.200 / 0.158\", q2_any: \"0.403 / 0.320 / 0.252\", r_any: \"25 / 31.5 / 40\", max_pressure_loss_at_q3: 0.25, length_footnotes: [\"2)\"], any_orientation_footnotes: [\"4)\"]}\n\nHmm, getting heavy. Simplify: rows as flat objects with the slash-strings; footnotes captured in table-level footnotes[] and per-row \"length_footnote_markers\": [\"2)\"].\n\nTable A2 (page 5, bearing a, Q3 = 16 and 25):\nRow: {q3: 16, q4: 20, q2_q1: 1.6, length_min: 270, length_footnote_markers: [\"3)\"], dn: 40, connection: \"G 2 B\", q1_H: \"0.400 / 0.320 / 0.254 / 0.200 / 0.160 / 0.128\", q2_H: \"0.640 / 0.512 / 0.406 / 0